{"reference":"Job 25","book":"Job","chapter":25,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Larger Debate","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Bildad’s short speech belongs to the sustained debate that follows Job’s lament. Job’s friends had first sat with him in silence for seven days, but they increasingly interpret his suffering as evidence of wrongdoing, while Job rejects that diagnosis. This larger setting cautions against treating Bildad’s words as an undifferentiated explanation of suffering; the book’s conclusion rebukes the friends’ speeches.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_025_7af6316b022629db05127f25"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Job and Endurance","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Later reflection connects Job with suffering, patience, endurance, and the Lord’s compassionate outcome. In this context, Job’s steadfastness concerns persistence across his story; it should not be taken to require silent endurance of abuse or to prevent seeking escape and protection.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_025_ad97f311f4654db79d17524d"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}